  "version_note": "1.3.0 differs from 1.2.0 in one number: the orientation proof's spatial cell edge, 4.0 voxels to 12.0. Measured on the control's own reference, 203103 triangles at a 4.0 cell need 23,478,019 index insertions against a cap of 4,000,000 -- a median of 120 cells per triangle, not a few outliers, because the cells are far finer than the triangles. The cap was doing its job; refusing to build a 23M-entry index inside a request is the point of it. At 12.0 the same reference needs 2,503,292 insertions, and the busiest cell holds about 40 triangles against a per-vertex candidate cap of 4096. The cell is an acceleration structure: it decides how many triangles are examined, never which one is nearest. What it does touch is the tie-break when two are equidistant, which is why this is a version and not an edit."
